<p>Corpus Christi wind damage claims come from more than hurricanes. Spring squall lines, tropical storms, and sudden downbursts all bring winds strong enough to strip shingles, peel metal roofing, knock down fences, and drive rain into walls. If you own a home or business in Corpus Christi, Nueces County, or nearby Coastal Bend communities, this page explains how wind claims usually work, what insurers look for, and how a licensed public adjuster may help.</p>
<p>Recent events show how intense local wind can be. On May 8, 2025, a line of severe storms produced National Weather Service estimated gusts of 105 to 115 mph around Petronila, Driscoll, and Agua Dulce in western Nueces County, snapping power poles and lifting mobile homes off their foundations (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1247377" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCEI event record</a>). During Tropical Storm Harold in August 2023, Corpus Christi International Airport recorded a gust of 62 mph, and tree and fence damage was reported in Flour Bluff. NOAA data show thunderstorm wind reports in Nueces County are most common in May, followed by March, June, and April.</p>

<h2>What Corpus Christi Wind Damage Claims Usually Involve</h2>
<p>Wind damage is not always obvious from the ground. Shingles can be lifted and creased, breaking the seal strip, and then lie back down looking almost normal. Damage we commonly see documented includes:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Roofing</strong> &ndash; missing or creased shingles, lifted ridge caps, displaced metal panels, and torn underlayment. See <a href="/services/roof-damage-insurance-claims/">roof damage insurance claims</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Exterior walls</strong> &ndash; loose or missing siding, cracked stucco, damaged fiber cement, and broken trim.</li>
<li><strong>Openings</strong> &ndash; broken windows, damaged garage doors, and torn screens.</li>
<li><strong>Wind-driven rain</strong> &ndash; water entering through a storm-created opening and damaging ceilings, insulation, drywall, and flooring.</li>
<li><strong>Fallen trees and debris</strong> &ndash; impact damage to roofs, cars, carports, and outbuildings.</li>
<li><strong>Other structures</strong> &ndash; fences, sheds, barns, and detached garages.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Which Policy Covers Wind on the Coastal Bend?</h2>
<p>This is the first question to answer. Nueces, San Patricio, Aransas, and Kleberg counties are all in the Texas Windstorm Insurance Association catastrophe area, and many homeowners policies there exclude wind and hail. If yours does, the wind claim goes to your TWIA policy, which has its own statutory deadlines. Our <a href="/services/twia-windstorm-claims/">TWIA windstorm claims</a> page explains that process. Inland properties, or properties with a private policy that includes wind, file with their regular carrier.</p>
