<h2>Documenting a Wind Damage Claim</h2>
<p>Good documentation connects the damage to a specific storm date and shows the full scope of what needs to be repaired.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Photograph and video</strong> the roof, all four elevations, and every damaged room before cleanup.</li>
<li><strong>Collect weather evidence</strong> such as NOAA Storm Events entries and NWS storm surveys. Our guide on <a href="/noaa-weather-records-document-property-damage-corpus-christi/">using NOAA weather records</a> explains how.</li>
<li><strong>Get a detailed inspection</strong> that records lifted or creased shingles, failed seals, and directional damage patterns.</li>
<li><strong>Prepare a line-item estimate</strong> covering roofing, exterior, interior, and any code items.</li>
<li><strong>Save receipts</strong> for tarping, board-up, tree removal, and water extraction.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> has a full list.</p>

<h2>Common Insurer Arguments on Wind Claims</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>"Wear and tear."</strong> Older roofs and salt-air corrosion on metal components are often cited as the cause instead of wind.</li>
<li><strong>"Not enough wind."</strong> The insurer may say measured winds were too low to cause the damage. Local data from nearby stations and NWS surveys can be important here.</li>
<li><strong>"Repairable, not replaceable."</strong> Spot repairs may be allowed even when shingles are brittle, discontinued, or cannot be matched.</li>
<li><strong>Interior damage excluded.</strong> The insurer may argue rain entered without a storm-created opening.</li>
<li><strong>Prior damage.</strong> The carrier may say the damage came from an earlier storm or a different policy period.</li>
</ul>
<p>If a wind claim has been denied or paid low, see our pages on <a href="/services/denied-insurance-claims/">denied insurance claims</a> and <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid insurance claims</a>.</p>

<h2>Codes, Permits, and Windstorm Certification</h2>
<p>Wind repairs in Corpus Christi often involve permits. The city requires a permit for re-roofing, and the roofing submission includes a TDI WPI-1 form from the windstorm engineer or inspector (<a href="https://www.corpuschristitx.gov/department-directory/development-services/applications-permits-and-guidance/residential-remodels-and-repairs/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">City of Corpus Christi</a>). The city's local design criteria list a design wind speed of 140/150 mph and a windborne debris zone. Code-driven costs are usually paid only under ordinance or law coverage, up to its limit.</p>

<h2>How a Corpus Christi Public Adjuster May Help</h2>
<p>A Corpus Christi Public Adjuster works for the policyholder. On a wind claim, that may include identifying which policy applies, inspecting the property, preparing a detailed estimate, gathering storm data, and communicating with the insurance adjuster on your behalf. If the insurer has already issued a payment, we can review the estimate line by line for missing scope.</p>
<p>We do not promise outcomes; every claim depends on the policy and the facts. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>

<h2>Policyholder Responsibilities After Wind Damage</h2>
<ul>
<li>Give prompt notice to your insurer, and to TWIA if wind is excluded from your homeowners policy.</li>
<li>Take reasonable steps to prevent more damage and keep the receipts.</li>
<li>Keep damaged materials when practical, or photograph them before disposal.</li>
<li>Respond to requests for information and submit any proof of loss on time.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you have questions about a lawsuit or deadlines to sue, consult a Texas attorney.</p>
